Whether there is a floating point unit is a question about the machine this
is being built for. mpg123's CMake build asks CMake about the machine it is
being built on -- and that query answers by reading the host's flags, which
on aarch64 says no. A cross build then compiles the fixed point decoder and
the NEON one together, which its own header refuses:

    #error "Bad decoder choice together with fixed point math!"

An answer given before the question is asked is kept, so that a build that
knows what it is building for can say so.

--- a/ports/cmake/src/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/ports/cmake/src/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -194,7 +194,9 @@
 check_cpu_arch_arm32(ARCH_IS_ARM32)
 check_cpu_arch_arm64(ARCH_IS_ARM64)
 
-if(WIN32 OR (ARCH_IS_ARM64 AND APPLE))
+if(DEFINED HAVE_FPU)
+    # Given by whoever configured this build, about the target.
+elseif(WIN32 OR (ARCH_IS_ARM64 AND APPLE))
     set(HAVE_FPU 1)
 else()
     cmake_host_system_information(RESULT HAVE_FPU QUERY HAS_FPU)
